WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.720
Know your true cost before you set a price.

2
00:00:03.070 --> 00:00:07.440
This is the video for Module 1 of Introduction to Pricing and Selling.

3
00:00:08.690 --> 00:00:11.020
Every sale has three numbers behind it.

4
00:00:11.370 --> 00:00:15.970
Cost is the money, and time, it takes you to make or buy the thing you sell.

5
00:00:17.220 --> 00:00:19.420
Price is what the customer pays you.

6
00:00:20.670 --> 00:00:24.130
Profit is what is left when you take cost away from price.

7
00:00:24.480 --> 00:00:33.350
If a pack of chin chin costs you 550 naira to make and you sell it for 800 naira, your profit is 250 naira.

8
00:00:34.600 --> 00:00:41.450
But if you sell it for 500 naira, you are losing 50 naira on every pack, even if your purse feels full.

9
00:00:41.800 --> 00:00:45.620
Money coming in is called revenue. Revenue is not profit.

10
00:00:46.870 --> 00:00:49.730
The first mistake is counting only materials.

11
00:00:50.080 --> 00:00:59.890
Many sellers add up flour, sugar and oil, then stop. They forget packaging, transport, gas, phone data, and most of all, their own hours of work.

12
00:01:01.140 --> 00:01:04.260
The second mistake is copying the next seller's price.

13
00:01:04.610 --> 00:01:12.690
She may buy in bulk, pay no rent, or be losing money without knowing it. Her price tells you about the market, not about your cost.

14
00:01:13.940 --> 00:01:16.760
The third mistake is never reviewing your price.

15
00:01:17.110 --> 00:01:26.050
Flour, cooking gas and transport can change quickly. A price that gave a good profit six months ago can quietly turn into a loss.

16
00:01:27.300 --> 00:01:37.410
Here is the method. First, add your direct costs for one batch. These are the things that go into or onto the product, like ingredients, gas and packaging.

17
00:01:37.760 --> 00:01:41.600
Use what you actually paid, from your receipts, not what you hope to pay.

18
00:01:42.850 --> 00:01:47.560
Second, add your labour. Ask what you could earn doing similar work for someone else.

19
00:01:47.910 --> 00:01:54.080
If a day's work of 8 hours pays about 8,000 naira, your hour is worth about 1,000 naira.

20
00:01:55.330 --> 00:02:06.460
Third, add a share of overhead. That is stall rent, market levy, fuel, data, transport and POS charges. Add them for a month and share them across everything you make.

21
00:02:07.710 --> 00:02:15.010
Fourth, allow for waste. If 2 of every 40 packs are spoiled or tasted, divide by 38, not 40.

22
00:02:16.260 --> 00:02:19.840
Finally, divide the batch total by the number of good items.

23
00:02:20.190 --> 00:02:24.480
The answer is your price floor. Below it, you lose money on every sale.

24
00:02:25.730 --> 00:02:33.920
Let us see this with Ngozi. She sells chin chin to offices and charges 500 naira a pack, because another seller does.

25
00:02:35.170 --> 00:02:41.190
Her ingredients and gas cost about 13,000 naira, and packaging about 2,000 naira.

26
00:02:42.440 --> 00:02:50.630
She works for 5 hours at 1,000 naira an hour, so 5,000 naira. Transport and data add 2,000 naira.

27
00:02:51.880 --> 00:02:58.200
Her batch costs 22,000 naira. Divided by 40, that is 550 naira a pack.

28
00:02:58.550 --> 00:03:05.780
At 500 naira she is giving away 50 naira of her labour on every sale. She is subsidising her customers.

29
00:03:07.030 --> 00:03:17.700
Before you change any price, protect every payment. A common scam is the fake alert: a screenshot or SMS says money was sent, but nothing has entered your account.

30
00:03:18.950 --> 00:03:27.270
Only trust money you can see in your balance inside your banking or wallet app. Check the amount and the sender name, then release the goods.

31
00:03:27.620 --> 00:03:33.670
Never share your PIN, password or OTP, even with someone who says they are from your bank.

32
00:03:34.920 --> 00:03:42.330
Set up these habits now. Keep business money in a separate account or wallet, and pay yourself a fixed wage on purpose.

33
00:03:42.680 --> 00:03:50.760
Keep three records: a cost sheet, a price list and a daily sales book. And keep your receipts, so the real numbers are always there.

34
00:03:52.010 --> 00:04:07.990
To remember. Revenue is not profit. Count every cost, including your own time. A competitor's price shows the market, not your cost. Your true cost per item is your price floor. And confirm the money in your balance before you hand over any goods.

35
00:04:09.240 --> 00:04:17.370
Now choose one product you sell most often. Write its current price and how you chose it, then work out its true cost on a cost sheet.

36
00:04:17.720 --> 00:04:23.030
Set up your record book with your own payment safety rule, and continue with the lessons below.
